\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Exercise 4.1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Suppose we pick some 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}\neq\ket{\psi_{0}}$
\end_inset

.
 How will the energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E=\Braket{\psi|\hat{H}|\psi}$
\end_inset

 compare to 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 must be greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the lowest allowed energy of the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 </details>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+end_note
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When quantum systems get large,
 the problem size gets large.
 Consider the following:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
For a single QCA molecule,
 the state vector is a two-dimensional vector with a 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times2$
\end_inset

 Hamiltonian:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\psi}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
c_{0}\\
c_{1}
\end{array}\right],\qquad\hat{H}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
H_{00} & H_{01}\\
H_{10} & H_{11}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For two QCA molecules,
 the state vector is 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{2}=4$
\end_inset

 dimensional,
 with a four-dimensional Hamiltonian
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 QCA molecules,
 the state vector is 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{N}$
\end_inset

 dimensional,
 with a 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{N}\times2^{N}$
\end_inset

 Hamiltonian
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
When the problem size becomes large,
 it may be advantageous to avoid diagonalizing 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This is where the variational quantum eigensolver (VQE) algorithm comes into play
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The VQE algorithm uses three key steps:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A parametric quantum circuit to transform 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Measurement is used to calculate system energy for a quantum system 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E=\Braket{\psi|\hat{H}|\psi}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $E>E_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 then we haven't found the ground state energy
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Since,
 in a VQE problem,
 we may not know the ground state,
 we don't necessarily know whether 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 is the ground state or not
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We can try another wave function 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi^{\prime}}$
\end_inset

,
 and compare its energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $E>E^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 for some new energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E^{\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 the we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 is not the ground state,
 since we found an 
\begin_inset Formula $E^{\prime}<E$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We don't know whether 
\begin_inset Formula $E^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is the ground state or not,
 so we can check another energy 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi^{\prime\prime}}$
\end_inset

 and its energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A classical optimizer modifies the parametric quantum circuit to generate a new 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The procedure repeats until it finds a minimum 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
