\begin_layout Itemize
Hopefully,
 we now recognize that we can transform 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 into any quantum state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 using only 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}_{z}\left(\theta_{z}\right)\mathbf{R}_{x}\left(\theta_{x}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
All the information about our arbitrary transformation 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}_{z}\left(\theta_{z}\right)\mathbf{R}_{x}\left(\theta_{x}\right)$
\end_inset

 is contained in the two parameters,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{x}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{z}$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 our circuit is called a 
\series bold
parametric quantum circuit
\series default
,
 since what it does depends on the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{x}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{z}$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For a more compact notation,
 we sometimes define a parameter vector,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{\theta}=\left(\theta_{x},\theta_{z}\right)$
\end_inset

 andrefer to our parameters collectively as 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{\theta}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This is one part of the VQE process:
 a parametric circuit,
 called an 
\series bold
ansatz
\series default
,
 transforms our standard quantum state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

,
 into some other state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

,
 called a 
\series bold
trial solution
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We often use the symbol 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{U}\left(\vec{\theta}\right)$
\end_inset

 to reference our parametric quantum circuit,
 so that the process by which we transform 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 is shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:AnsatzOnly"

\end_inset


